  I think many people are not clear on exactly what the work would
entail. For instance, the documentation writer position. This
person would be primarily responsible for writing short files
on list commands (the help system). Harry paid Tony Hamilton
the same fee for the current list help docs (try looking at
them, ::help exclude, or just ::help)  Basically, for about 10-15 hours
work, you are getting $200. That's over $10/hr, hardly small
pay for an intern. Or consider the C++ intern. At most, I would
be asking someone to write a small object to do a very specialized
function, say convert an RFC1183 (standard digest) into a MIME Digest.
Again, the work is undemanding. As the person doing most of the
work (for basically nothing at this point), $200 is the true value
of the work we are asking for. It is my opportunity cost. If we had
to pay much more, I'd do the accessory objects myself and Harry would
write the docs. And the research position is even easier. Harry
is going to pay to $200 to get someone to go netsurfing? Again,
if he had to pay any more, I'd go do it myself.

   I guess Harry shouldn't have used the word "intern" since it
conjurs up images of grad students working grueling workloads
for nothing.  We are looking for part-time contract workers. They are
interns in the sense that we would like to keep them with the company
in a loose relationship since if we are successful, we will need
some help providing tech support, and someone who has written docs
or did some programming work will know how the product works and be
an excellent person for fielding questions.


