From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Thu, 15 Sep 1994 15:35:23 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-148] Superheros

What are the most Extropian comic book characters?
For example, there are a host of popular characters that
implement self-transformation in various ways: Superman, 
Spiderman, X-Men, Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles, Atom Boy,
etc.  The core theme of superheros is their ability
to transform themselves into something better than human.
Strong resonances to Nietchze's "ubermensch", and our own
goals of self transformation.  Contrary to much of our
culture, ranging from cyberpunk literature to medical
ethics, the dramatic self improvement of comic book
heros is often portrayed as quite attractive and
desirable, rather than as an ugly grim necessity (cyberpunk)
an evil to be stamped out (contemporary medical ethics).

An interesting discussion between Dave Krieger, myself,
and several other folks a few weeks ago revolved around 
the fantastic nature of many of the superheros' attributes,
which make them impossible to directly implement in the
real world.  The first one mentioned was Superman's ability to listen
through vacuum.  Despite the gross violation of physics of the most
straightforward interpretation of this skill, there are
ways to indirectly implement it in some situations (eg spies use 
lasers to listen in on people's rooms via the window vibrations).  

Superheros might be a goldmine of ideas for both popular, and, with
clever modifications, feasible forms of self transformation.
Another example: utility fog reminds me of Sandman, a character 
who could turn himself into sand and "leak" out of jail cells and 
locked rooms.  Such ideas are much more attractive than bleak
portrayals of self transformation that tend to accompany descriptions
of cyborgs, brain implants, and the like.  With superheros we can 
put some spotlight on the attractive results instead of focusing 
on the gory details of implementation, which I have observed lend 
themselves to scare mongering and turn off the public to the concept 
of radically improving ourselves.

